C o m p i l a t i o n


      Reservoir Design Problem - Mixed Integer Programming HW5
      CEE 6410/5410 by Dr.David Rosenberg
       
      -------------------------------
      Problem text from canvas
      -------------------------------
      Formulate and Solve the mixed integer program for Chapter 7, Problem #1 (a 3rd change, this one final)
      Technical portion of the grading rubric:
      Integer programming
      a) Problem Formulation - Dimensions, Decision Variables (10 pts), Objective Function (10 pts), Constraints (10 pts)
      b) Solution Method (20)
      c) Results, Recommendations, and New Insights for Management (20)
       
      -------------------------------
      Problem Statement 
      -------------------------------
      A farmer plans to develop water for irrigation. He is considering two possible sources of
      water: a gravity diversion from a possible reservoir with two alternative capacities and/or a
      pump from a lower river diversion (refer to Figure 7.3). Between the reservoir and pump
      site the river base flow increases by 2 acft/day due to groundwater drainage into the river.
      Ignore losses from the reservoir. The river flow into the reservoir and the farmer's demand
      during each of two six-month seasons of the year are given in Table 7.5. Revenue is estimated
      at $300 per year per acre irrigated.
       
       
      Assume that there are only two possible sizes of reservoir: a high dam that has capacity of
      700 acft or a low dam with capacity of 300 acft. The capital costs are $10,000/year and
      $6,000/year for the high and low dams, respectively (no operating cost). The pump capacity
      is fixed at 2.2 acft/day with a capital cost (if it is built) of $8,000/year and operating cost
      of $20/acft.
       
       
      Table 7.5
      Season,t     River Inflow, Qt (acft)    Irrigation Demand (acft/acre)    
      1            600                        1.0
      2            200                        3.0
       
      Table 7.6
      Facility            Capital ($/year)    Operating Cost ($/acft)  Capacity (acft)
      Reservior High      10000               0                        700
      Reservior Low       6000                0                        300
      Pump                8000                20                       2.2acft/day
       
      Full details at https://usu.instructure.com/courses/818402/files/100525844?wrap=1
       
       
      Author
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      October 19, 2026
       
  54   
  55  *------House Keeping-------------------------
  56  *Number of seasons:     2
  57  *Season Length:         6months
  58  *Pump Capacity/season:  2.2*30*6  =           396 acft/season
  59  *Ground Water Drainage: 2acft/day = 2*30*60 = 360 acft/season
  60   
  61   
  62  *------1. DEFINE the SETS or Dimensions-------
  63  SETS
  64      t Seasons          /1,2/
  65      src Water Sources  /Dam1, Dam2, Pump/
  66  *    Dam1 is High capacity dam and Dam2 is low capacity dam
  67                      ;
  68   
  69   
  70  *------2. DEFINE input data-------
  71   
  72  PARAMETERS
  73   
  74      Q(t)           River inflow (acft)               /1 600,2 200/
  75      Demand(t)      Irrigation Demand (acft per acre) /1 1,2 3/
  76      Capacity(src)  Source Capacity (acft)            /Dam1 700, Dam2 300, Pump 396/
  77      Cost(src)      Capital Cost ($)                  /Dam1 10000, Dam2 6000, Pump 8000/
  78      OpCost(src)    Operational Cost($ per acft)      /Dam1 0, Dam2 0, Pump 20/
  79      Revenue        Irrigation Revenue ($ per acre)   /300/
  80      GroundDrainage Ground Water Drainage (per season)/360/;
  81   
  82   
  83  *------3. DEFINE the variables-------
  84  Variables
  85      Z           Profit ($)
  86      AC          Acres irrigated by water
  87      R(t)        Water released from reservior in season t (acft per season)
  88      Sp(t)       Water spill in river from reservior
  89      P(t)        Water pumped from pump in season t (acft per season)
  90      S(t)        Reservior storage at the end of season t (acft)
  91      I(src)      Binary variable for the source choices;
  92   
  93  Binary Variable I;
  94  Positive Variables Sp,AC,R,P,S;
  95   
  96  *------4. COMBINE variables and data in equations-------
  97  *EQUATIONS
  98  Equations
  99     Profit          Total Profit ($) and obj. function
 100     MB1             Mass Balance at the end of season 1
 101     MB2             Mass Balance at the end of season 2
 102     StCap1          Storage Capacity in season 1
 103     StCap2          Storage Capacity in season 2
 104     Demand1         Water Demand in season 1
 105     Demand2         Water Demand in season 2
 106     DamSelect       Dam type choice
 107     PumpCap1        Pump Capacity in season 1
 108     PumpCap2        Pump Capacity in season 2
 109     Pwater1         water pumped in season 1
 110     Pwater2         water pumped in season 2
 111   
 112   
 113  ;
 114   
 115  *------Objective Function -------
 116  *Maximizing Profit = Benefits - Costs
 117  Profit..              Revenue*AC - Cost('Dam1')*I('Dam1') -Cost('Dam2')*I('Dam2') - Cost('Pump')*I('Pump') - OpCost('Pump')*Sum(t,P(t)) =e=  Z ;
 118  MB1..                 S('2') =e= S('1') + Q('1')-Sp('1')-R('1');
 119  MB2..                 S('1') =e= S('2') + Q('2')-Sp('2')-R('2');
 120  StCap1..              S('1') =L= Capacity('Dam1')*I('Dam1') + Capacity('Dam2')*I('Dam2');
 121  StCap2..              S('2') =L= Capacity('Dam1')*I('Dam1') + Capacity('Dam2')*I('Dam2');
 122  Demand1..             R('1')+P('1') =G= 1*AC;
 123  Demand2..             R('2')+P('2') =G= 3*AC;
 124  DamSelect..           I('Dam1')+I('Dam2')=L=1;
 125  PumpCap1..            P('1') =L= Capacity('Pump')*I('Pump');
 126  PumpCap2..            P('2') =L= Capacity('Pump')*I('Pump');
 127  Pwater1..             P('1') =L= Sp('1')+GroundDrainage;
 128  Pwater2..             P('2') =L= Sp('2')+GroundDrainage;
 129   
 130   
 131  **Constraints
 132  *CapLimit(i)..         BusCapacity * Buses(i)                          =l=  Capacity(i) ;
 133  *TTimeLimit..          sum(i, TravelTime(i) * Buses(i) * BusCapacity)  =l=  MaxAverageTime*Attendees;
 134  *GSLimitSeats..        sum(j, GS(j) * Seats(j))                        =g=  Attendees;
 135  *VendorLink(j)..       GS(j)  =l= (Attendees/Seats(j)) * VSelect(j);
 136  *RshipLimit..          sum(i, Buses(i) * BusCapacity)                   =g=  Attendees;
 137   
 138  *------5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS -------
 139  model WaterDistribution /all/;
 140   
 141   
 142  *------6. Solve the Model as a Mixed Integer Program -------
 143  solve WaterDistribution using mip maximize Z;
 144   
 145  *------7. Disparklotay the decision variable values in the list file -------
 146  *display GS.L, Buses.L, Z.L;
 147  *
 148  ** Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
 149  *Execute_Unload "MIP-HW5_Talha.gdx";
 150  ** Dump the gdx file to an Excel workbook
 151  *Execute "gdx2xls MIP-HW5_Talha.gdx"


